WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:04.840
Some knitters will choose to use a heavier-

2
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.040
than-recommended yarn on their
standard gauge machine.

3
00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:14.800
Remember, the criteria for the correct
yarn to use on your machine has to do

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.440
with the yarn being able to fit
in the hooks of the needles.

5
00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.440
And there has to be enough
room between the needles.

6
00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:24.160
So the stitches form properly.

7
00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:30.360
And often-used trick is to knit with every
other needle. You still have to swatch,

8
00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:35.440
but your swatch may not be knit  according
to the rules for a standard gauge machine.

9
00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:41.600
Normally, we would measure 40 stitches
and 60 rows with a green ruler.

10
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:43.480
For this example, we're going to measure

11
00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:49.000
over our choice of stitches and rows and use
a regular ruler to calculate our gauge.

12
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:50.720
Take a look at my swatch.

13
00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:54.480
I've marked and measured over 20 stitches.

14
00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:58.560
I've knit 60 rows between the row markers.

15
00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:03.720
Just looking at the swatch,
you don't know what machine I used and you

16
00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:09.560
certainly can't tell that I knit
this swatch over every other needle.

17
00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:12.520
The point is, no matter what your needle

18
00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:17.880
arrangement, we are counting
stitches, not needles.

19
00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:21.400
Take a look at the same
swatch on the machine,

20
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:27.280
I pulled 73 needles
and then I put 38 needles into work

21
00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:32.160
and put every other needle
into out of work position.

22
00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:34.920
I did an e-wrap Cast-on and started

23
00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:40.400
knitting.
And as I knit, I was forming 38 total

24
00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:46.440
stitches and marked 20 to use
for my swatch measurement.

25
00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:50.280
When I took the knitting off the machine,
it didn't look like much.

26
00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:52.120
You can see the channels between

27
00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.720
the stitches and the stitches
look really tiny.

28
00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:01.760
I grabbed a swatch by the Cast-on
and Bindoff edges and gave it a tug.

29
00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:05.560
This reformed the stitches
and they look great.

30
00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:09.160
Don't be afraid of stretching too much.

31
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:15.680
But with all that tugging and stretching,
you must let that poor swatch rest.

32
00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:21.760
At least overnight, before
you block it and measure it.

33
00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:26.080
To really get a feel for the importance
of letting a swatch rest.

34
00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.800
Please work this exercise.

35
00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:35.720
Knit a swatch and measure
it right off the machine.

36
00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:41.800
Stretch the knitting lengthwise to pop
and reform those stitches and measure it

37
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.040
again. You're going
to see a huge difference.

38
00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:51.800
Let the swatch rest for a few
hours and measure again.

39
00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:54.840
Let it rest overnight at least,

40
00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:00.800
and measure it again, writing
down the measurements each time.

41
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.640
Then block your swatch treat it as you're

42
00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:09.240
going to treat your final
garment and measure.

43
00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:15.240
You're going to see a big difference
in the gauge each step of the way.

44
00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:19.920
I hope you won't be tempted to start
knitting based on the gauge

45
00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:25.880
of the knitting taken right off
the machine without letting it rest.

46
00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:29.880
Please don't get confused when
you're knitting every other needle.

47
00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:35.040
You're measuring and counting stitches,
not needles.

48
00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:38.960
So keep that in mind when you're following
the knitting instructions as well.

